riscv32: don't pad 64-bit syscall arguments to an even register pair

The RISC-V Linux syscall ABI passes a 64-bit argument in the next two
registers with no padding (the kernel reads ftruncate's length from a1/a2).
With the ARM-style alignment uClibc sent (fd, 0, low, high), so the kernel
saw a length of 0 and, for example, busybox vi truncated every file it saved.

--- a/libc/sysdeps/linux/riscv32/bits/uClibc_arch_features.h
+++ b/libc/sysdeps/linux/riscv32/bits/uClibc_arch_features.h
@@ -13,7 +13,7 @@
 /* does your target use statx */
 #undef __UCLIBC_HAVE_STATX__
 
-#define __UCLIBC_SYSCALL_ALIGN_64BIT__
+#undef __UCLIBC_SYSCALL_ALIGN_64BIT__
 
 /* does your target have a broken create_module() ? */
 #define __UCLIBC_BROKEN_CREATE_MODULE__
